    "preparation": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "preparation",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Research and implement a generic output-path optimization. The current PrintPermutation emits one line per permutation with std::endl, forcing a flush each time. Compare newline-only output and a bounded buffered writer; preserve the exact `[ `, character-and-space, `]` format and line order for every input. Do not accumulate the entire output without a memory bound.",
        "B": "Profile and investigate the sequential path: input whitespace removal and sorting, then the in-place lexicographic successor loop. The current code already avoids storing permutations and handles repeated characters; seek a measurable generic improvement without changing output order or semantics. Compare alternatives against the existing traversal and avoid replacing it with a materialized permutation list. If the path is not a meaningful bottleneck, report that rather than adding complexity.",
        "C": "Investigate whether parallel generation can help on the 4-core ARM64 target. A possible design is independent blocks grouped by distinct first character, with bounded buffering and ordered output/merge so the result remains globally lexicographic. Preserve duplicate handling and exact formatting; prevent interleaved output and unbounded memory use. Measure overhead and retain a serial approach if parallelization is not beneficial.",
        "G": "Integrate only candidates from A/B/C that are correct and show measured benefit. Check exact output against the baseline, including lexicographic ordering, repeated symbols, whitespace handling, and formatting. Benchmark candidates individually and after integration: combined performance may be worse than either input, so remeasure the integrated program and keep only changes supported by those results.",
        "analysis": "The supplied baseline is correct on 4 cases, with mean 0.1347666417 s and three runs between 0.1346353926 and 0.1348606981 s. The current source enumerates permutations in place, but flushes with std::endl on every emitted permutation, making output buffering the clearest initial target. The output must still contain every distinct permutation in lexicographic order; no test-specific shortcuts or omitted computation are valid. The target has 4 CPU cores and no GPU."
      },
