    "synthesis": {
      "model_calls": 1,
      "phase": "synthesis",
      "plan": {
        "A": "Focus on output throughput. The fastest measured candidate used a fixed 64 KiB chunked writer (0.00644 s versus the 0.13477 s baseline). Validate its exact formatting and correctness, then test writer changes one at a time; keep memory bounded and preserve enumeration order. Do not assume a larger buffer or a combined candidate is faster.",
        "B": "Focus on the permutation traversal hot path: compare the existing suffix/pivot/successor/reverse loop with carefully optimized alternatives. Preserve lexicographic order and emit each distinct multiset permutation exactly once, including with repeated characters. Measure end-to-end as well as traversal cost, since output may dominate.",
        "C": "Investigate input cleanup, string copies, and initial sorting as a separate, lower-priority path. The measured copy-removal change was essentially at baseline (0.13642 s), and the OpenMP counting-sort candidate measured 0.01512 s, slower than the best output-buffer candidate; neither supports assuming preprocessing or parallel sorting is a win. Seek only generic, correctness-preserving improvements that show end-to-end gains, especially on cases where little output is produced.",
        "G": "Validate candidate correctness before integration, then benchmark the best candidates and combinations on the full input set. Treat the 64 KiB writer as the strongest measured lead, not a guaranteed final winner. Changes may interact, so compare each integrated result against its inputs rather than adding their apparent gains; retain exact output and all valid-input behavior.",
        "analysis": "The source enumerates all distinct lexicographic permutations and prints each one with per-character stream insertions and std::endl. The verified baseline mean is 0.13477 s. The supplied measurements favor A's chunked writer (0.00644 s); B's line buffer measured 0.00805 s, while newline-only and the counting-sort candidate measured about 0.015 s. The copy-removal result was 0.13642 s. These are leads, not proof of candidate correctness or additive gains; require correctness checks and end-to-end measurements for every integration."
